   "texts": {
    "profile": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: Turning Light into Sugar\n\nINTRO: Photosynthesis is how plants make glucose, the sugar they use for energy and growth. This short lesson traces the inputs, the light catcher, and the outputs; a couple of quick recalls will show what stuck.\n\nSECTION 1 - The Core Rule: Carbon Dioxide, Water, Light\nPlants make glucose, a sugar, from carbon dioxide and water. They use light energy from the sun to drive that change. Picture the arrow chain: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen. Takeaway: carbon dioxide and water are the raw materials, and light energy powers the change.\nEXAMPLE: Try it: In a comic panel, a leaf has arrows for two raw materials coming in and light hitting it. Which two raw materials does the plant take in to make glucose? Answer: carbon dioxide and water.\n\nSECTION 2 - The Site: Leaves, Chloroplasts, Chlorophyll\nPhotosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves, inside cells that contain chloroplasts. Chloroplasts hold chlorophyll, the green pigment that absorbs light. That absorbed light energy powers the conversion of carbon dioxide and water into glucose. Takeaway: leaves are the main site, and chlorophyll catches the light.\nEXAMPLE: Try it: A comic artist colors a leaf green. Which pigment gives that green color, and where is it stored? Answer: chlorophyll, in the chloroplasts.\n\nSECTION 3 - The Balanced Picture: Glucose and Oxygen\nThe overall equation is 6 CO2 + 6 H2O + light energy -> C6H12O6 + 6 O2. Count the atoms: 6 carbon, 12 hydrogen, and 18 oxygen on each side. The glucose stores energy the plant uses for growth, and oxygen is released as a by-product. Takeaway: the equation balances, and oxygen leaves the plant.\nEXAMPLE: Try it: In that equation, how many oxygen molecules are released for each glucose molecule? Answer: 6.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: In a video game, a leafy plant sits in a clear sealed room with carbon dioxide and water, but the room's light switch is off for a day. Has the plant made new glucose by photosynthesis? Use the core rule to explain.\n  1. List what the rule requires: carbon dioxide, water, and light energy.\n  2. Check the materials: carbon dioxide and water are present in the room.\n  3. Check the energy: the room is dark, so chlorophyll cannot absorb light energy.\n  4. Apply the rule: without absorbed light energy, the plant cannot convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose.\n  5. Conclusion: no new glucose is made by photosynthesis, and oxygen is not released as a by-product.\n  ANSWER: No. The materials are present, but light energy is missing, so photosynthesis cannot run.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: Plants make glucose from carbon dioxide and oxygen, and they release water. Why: oxygen is the by-product released, not an input; water is an input, not a product. Right: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen.\n\nSUMMARY: Photosynthesis turns carbon dioxide and water into glucose using light energy. Chlorophyll in leaf chloroplasts absorbs the light, and oxygen is released as a by-product.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. Which pigment absorbs the light energy used in photosynthesis? [choices: Chlorophyll | Hemoglobin | Melanin | Keratin] -> Chlorophyll\n2. A plant has carbon dioxide, water, and plenty of light. Name the sugar it produces and the gas it releases. [typed answer] -> glucose and oxygen",
    "mirror": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: How Plants Make Their Own Food\n\nINTRO: A plant cannot go and buy lunch. It has to build its own food inside itself, and that job is called photosynthesis. This lesson shows the ingredients, the energy, and where the job happens.\n\nSECTION 1 - The Rule: Two Ingredients, One Food\nRule: a plant makes its own food, and that food is a sugar called glucose. It builds glucose from two ingredients: carbon dioxide (a gas in the air) and water. Carbon dioxide gets in through tiny holes in the leaves, and water comes up from the roots. Put them together with light energy and you get glucose plus oxygen, like this: carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen.\nEXAMPLE: Picture it as an arrow chain: carbon dioxide (from the air) + water (from the soil) -> glucose (the plant's food) + oxygen (given out). A tomato plant in a sunny garden runs this chain all day long.\n\nSECTION 2 - The Rule: Chlorophyll Catches the Light\nRule: a plant cannot use sunlight directly, so it first catches the light with chlorophyll. Chlorophyll is the green colouring inside a leaf, and it sits in tiny parts of leaf cells called chloroplasts. Chlorophyll soaks up the light energy and hands it to the glucose-building job. No chlorophyll means no light energy caught, and no glucose made.\nEXAMPLE: Think of a basketball pass: the sunlight is the ball flying in, and chlorophyll is the pair of hands that catches it. A plant in a dark cupboard still has the hands, but no ball ever arrives. Try it: A leaf is green. Which substance makes it green, and which tiny parts of the cell hold that substance? Answer: chlorophyll makes it green, and chloroplasts hold it.\n\nSECTION 3 - The Rule: Leaves Do the Job and Oxygen Comes Out\nRule: photosynthesis (the name for this whole glucose-building job) happens mainly in the leaves. Leaves are flat and thin, so they catch plenty of light and let gases move in and out easily. The oxygen made along the way is a by-product, which means it is the leftover, not the point of the job. The plant releases that oxygen into the air, and animals, including you, breathe it in.\nEXAMPLE: Think of a comic panel: a leaf in the middle, light arrows hitting it from above, carbon dioxide arrows going in, oxygen arrows puffing out, and water arrows coming up the stem. That one panel shows the whole job. Try it: A leaf has water, carbon dioxide and light, but all its chlorophyll is gone. Which step fails, and what is the result? Answer: chlorophyll cannot catch the light, so no light energy is captured and no glucose is made.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Maya ties a black paper bag over one leaf of a healthy plant and leaves it for two days. The plant still gets water and air. Does that covered leaf make glucose? Work it through step by step.\n  1. Step 1: list the ingredients from the rule. Glucose needs carbon dioxide, water and light energy.\n  2. Step 2: check carbon dioxide. The leaf is still in the air, so carbon dioxide can still get in through the holes in the leaf. That ingredient is there.\n  3. Step 3: check water. The roots still take up water and it travels up the stem to the leaf. That ingredient is there.\n  4. Step 4: check light. The black bag blocks the sunlight, so no light reaches the leaf.\n  5. Step 5: link light to chlorophyll. Chlorophyll in the chloroplasts is still in the leaf, but it has no light to catch, so it captures no light energy.\n  6. Step 6: apply the rule. With no light energy captured, the carbon dioxide and water cannot be turned into glucose.\n  ANSWER: No. The covered leaf makes no new glucose, because the light energy is missing, even though water and carbon dioxide are both there.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: Plants eat soil, so the food a plant makes is soil and water. Why: the ingredient step is wrong, because soil is not the food; soil supplies water and minerals, and water is only one ingredient. Right: the food is glucose, built from carbon dioxide and water using light energy.\nWrong: Oxygen is the plant's food, because plants give off oxygen. Why: the output step is flipped; oxygen is the leftover by-product that is released, not the food. Right: glucose is the food, and oxygen is released as a by-product.\nWrong: Photosynthesis happens in the roots, because that is where water goes in. Why: taking in water is not the same as making food; the food is made where chlorophyll is. Right: water travels up from the roots, and glucose is made mainly in the leaves.\n\nSUMMARY: A plant makes glucose from carbon dioxide and water, using light energy from the sun. Chlorophyll in the chloroplasts of leaf cells catches that light. Oxygen is released as a by-product, and the whole job happens mainly in the leaves.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. Which gas does a plant take in from the air to make glucose? Hint: it is the gas you breathe out. [choices: Carbon dioxide | Oxygen | Nitrogen | Water vapour] -> Carbon dioxide\n2. A plant has plenty of water and carbon dioxide but sits in a dark cupboard. Does it make glucose? Type no or yes, then name the missing ingredient. Hint: think about what chlorophyll needs to catch. [typed answer] -> No. Light (light energy) is missing.\n3. Which list has everything a plant must take in from outside itself to make glucose? Hint: one item is a form of energy, not a material. [choices: Light, carbon dioxide, water | Light, soil, oxygen | Carbon dioxide, oxygen, water | Glucose, water, light] -> Light, carbon dioxide, water"
   }
  }
